{
  "id": "97d1309a-6739-4ff9-a354-f0c6859df60f",
  "kind": "Theorem",
  "definition_keys": [],
  "heading": null,
  "heading_latex": "\\textrm{A Partition Determines an Equivalence Relation}",
  "body_text": null,
  "proof_text": "Let $P$ be a partition of $X$. Define a relation $R$ by\n$x \\mathrel{R} y$ if and only if there exists a block $A \\in P$ such\nthat $x \\in A$ and $y \\in A$. We verify that $R$ is an equivalence\nrelation:\n1. *Reflexivity*: Because $P$ partitions $X$, every $x \\in X$ belongs\n   to some block $A \\in P$. Since $x \\in A$ and $x \\in A$, we have\n   $x \\mathrel{R} x$.\n2. *Symmetry*: If $x \\mathrel{R} y$, there is some $A \\in P$ with\n   $x, y \\in A$. Since $y, x \\in A$, we have $y \\mathrel{R} x$.\n3. *Transitivity*: If $x \\mathrel{R} y$ and $y \\mathrel{R} z$, there\n   exist blocks $A, B \\in P$ such that $x, y \\in A$ and $y, z \\in B$.\n   Then $y \\in A \\cap B$, so $A \\cap B \\neq \\emptyset$. Because\n   distinct blocks of a partition are mutually disjoint, $A = B$. Thus\n   $x, z \\in A$, which proves $x \\mathrel{R} z$. Hence $R$ is an\n   equivalence relation on $X$.",
  "proof_source": "Proof: \"\"\"Let $P$ be a partition of $X$. Define a relation $R$ by\n          $x \\mathrel{R} y$ if and only if there exists a block $A \\in P$ such\n          that $x \\in A$ and $y \\in A$. We verify that $R$ is an equivalence\n          relation:\n          1. *Reflexivity*: Because $P$ partitions $X$, every $x \\in X$ belongs\n             to some block $A \\in P$. Since $x \\in A$ and $x \\in A$, we have\n             $x \\mathrel{R} x$.\n          2. *Symmetry*: If $x \\mathrel{R} y$, there is some $A \\in P$ with\n             $x, y \\in A$. Since $y, x \\in A$, we have $y \\mathrel{R} x$.\n          3. *Transitivity*: If $x \\mathrel{R} y$ and $y \\mathrel{R} z$, there\n             exist blocks $A, B \\in P$ such that $x, y \\in A$ and $y, z \\in B$.\n             Then $y \\in A \\cap B$, so $A \\cap B \\neq \\emptyset$. Because\n             distinct blocks of a partition are mutually disjoint, $A = B$. Thus\n             $x, z \\in A$, which proves $x \\mathrel{R} z$. Hence $R$ is an\n             equivalence relation on $X$.\"\"\"",
  "page": null,
  "source": "Theorem:\ngiven:\n. X is \\set\n. P is \\partition:of{X}\nthen:\n. exists: R is \\equivalence.relation:on{X}\n  suchThat:\n  . forAll: x, y \"in\" X\n    then:\n    . have: R(x, y)\n      iff:\n      . exists: A is \\set\n        suchThat:\n        . A \"in\"? P\n        . x \"in\"? A\n        . y \"in\"? A\nDocumented:\n. called: \"A Partition Determines an Equivalence Relation\"\n. description: \"Two elements are equivalent precisely when they belong to the\n                same block of the partition.\"\nProof: \"\"\"Let $P$ be a partition of $X$. Define a relation $R$ by\n          $x \\mathrel{R} y$ if and only if there exists a block $A \\in P$ such\n          that $x \\in A$ and $y \\in A$. We verify that $R$ is an equivalence\n          relation:\n          1. *Reflexivity*: Because $P$ partitions $X$, every $x \\in X$ belongs\n             to some block $A \\in P$. Since $x \\in A$ and $x \\in A$, we have\n             $x \\mathrel{R} x$.\n          2. *Symmetry*: If $x \\mathrel{R} y$, there is some $A \\in P$ with\n             $x, y \\in A$. Since $y, x \\in A$, we have $y \\mathrel{R} x$.\n          3. *Transitivity*: If $x \\mathrel{R} y$ and $y \\mathrel{R} z$, there\n             exist blocks $A, B \\in P$ such that $x, y \\in A$ and $y, z \\in B$.\n             Then $y \\in A \\cap B$, so $A \\cap B \\neq \\emptyset$. Because\n             distinct blocks of a partition are mutually disjoint, $A = B$. Thus\n             $x, z \\in A$, which proves $x \\mathrel{R} z$. Hence $R$ is an\n             equivalence relation on $X$.\"\"\"\nId: \"97d1309a-6739-4ff9-a354-f0c6859df60f\"",
